# Task 2 - where the shuffle is, and what it costs.
#
# The readings arrive long: one row per sensor per timestamp. The question
# asked of them is per room, so at some point every reading for a room has to
# end up on the same machine. That move is the shuffle, and it is the only
# expensive thing in this pipeline.
#
# Narrow steps keep each partition where it is: a machine can do its share
# without talking to anyone. Wide steps cannot — they have to redistribute the
# data by key first, and every machine waits for that to finish. On the diagram
# a wide step is the one with a barrier in front of it.
#
# Reshaping long data into wide is a wide step for exactly this reason, and
# that is the answer the report asks you for: not that pivoting is slow, but
# why it has to be.
#
# Try these in order. Each step is marked on the line it belongs to.
#
#   1. run it as it is. Read the stages off the diagram and say, before you
#      change anything, which line caused the barrier
#   2. drop the readings you do not need earlier instead of later, and see
#      whether the shuffle gets cheaper
#   3. lose the grouped step and watch what has to be rebuilt. Compare that
#      with losing the step before it
#   4. make one executor slow. The stage takes as long as its slowest
#      machine, which is what a straggler means
#   5. ask yourself which of these steps could have run at the same time

# --- the machines ----------------------------------------------------

@machine
class Executor:
    pass

e1 = Executor(speed=1.0)
e2 = Executor(speed=1.0)
e3 = Executor(speed=1.0)      # step 4: make it 0.3

# --- the world -------------------------------------------------------

world = World(machines=[e1, e2, e3])

# --- the pipeline ----------------------------------------------------
# Each line says what it came from. That is the lineage.

readings = textFile("telemetry.csv")
parsed = readings.map(split(value))
# step 2: move this filter up, so it runs before the pair is built
warm = parsed.filter(value)
byRoom = warm.mapToPair(value, 1)
grouped = byRoom.groupByKey()
perRoom = grouped.mapValues(sum(value))

job = Spark(pipeline=perRoom, lose=grouped)   # step 3: lose=byRoom

world.run(job)
